- id: D261108-fyhht
  timestamp: 2026-11-08T09:28:22Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-08T10:44:43Z
  summary: 'Adopt six core analysis rules: a change is measured from the merge-base, analysis never writes into the reviewed repo, name-matched references always carry a warning, a failed LLM reply keeps the deterministic report, every finding is anchored to something on the map, and formatting-only changes ask for no review.'
  rationale: 'These are the guarantees a reviewer relies on when reading a map, and all six already hold in the code, so writing them down records current behavior and lets statute flag changes that would break them. Rejected for now: per-branch reviewed marks (viewer convenience, not core), refusing reports older than version 4 (no enforcing code found), and byte-identical reports for the same inputs (not verified; warnings are stored in a set). CORE-003 deliberately puts the guarantee on the per-language warnings rather than analyzer.resolution, which reads index when any language was indexed.'
  establishes:
  - CORE-001
  - CORE-002
  - CORE-003
  - CORE-004
  - CORE-005
  - CORE-006
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261115-bbchl
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T11:33:30Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-15T20:30:15Z
  summary: 'Change CORE-003: the main change report never matches references by name. A language without a usable index (the build failed, indexing was cancelled, or indexes are off), and every file an index misses, stays syntax-only with a warning that names the language and the reason. unused-api, orphaned and untested run only when the index covers every file that could hold the reference. Name matching stays in the labelled comparison report.'
  rationale: 'The user''s goal is that everything the review shows is true. Name matching produced the false positives that made index mode the prototype''s default, and as a fallback it showed those links again exactly when the index failed. Chosen by the user on 2026-11-15: syntax-only also for partial indexes and with indexes turned off, and the checks held back unless coverage is complete, accepting that untested rarely runs when test targets are outside the review scope and that an uncovered file anywhere holds the checks back. Rejected: keeping name matching for uncovered files; letting the absence of a name in uncovered files stand in for coverage, which misses implicit uses such as operators and delegates. The comparison report stays because the rebuild proposal asks for indexed and non-indexed results side by side during validation. The prototype (Sources/) keeps the old behaviour as the comparison baseline, so its CORE-003 links are removed. Conformers and overrides still resolve supertype names from inheritance clauses in indexed languages; that is recorded as a gap, and they are held back for syntax-only languages.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-003
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261116-29htm
  timestamp: 2026-11-16T07:12:40Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-16T07:18:10Z
  summary: 'CORE-002 no longer protects the case of an LLM cache directory inside the reviewed repository: the cache''s location can''t be chosen anymore, so the case can''t arise. The rule itself, that analysis never writes into the reviewed repository, is unchanged.'
  rationale: 'The prototype had an --llm-cache flag, so a reviewer could point the cache into the repository, and the annotator refused that with an error. The rebuild first kept the option as LlmOptions.cacheDir, used only so tests could keep their caches apart, and refused a cache inside the repository with an llm-unavailable warning, which no longer matched the case''s wording. The user asked to remove the option instead (2026-11-16): replies are always cached in the app''s own cache (<cache root>/llm), and keyed by the command among the rest, so each test keeps its replies apart by running a fake of its own. Rejected: rewording the case to the warning, which would keep a test-only setting and a rule for a situation no user flow reaches. Rejected for now: one check at the start of a review that refuses a cache or data directory inside the repository, which only REVIEWMAP_HOME, a variable for tests and development, could cause.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-002
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261116-2s3v0
  timestamp: 2026-11-16T07:47:35Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-16T08:05:41Z
  summary: 'Change CORE-003: supertypes come only from the index in the main report. The conformers check and conformer and override context use the declaration the index resolves each inheritance-clause entry to; an entry the index does not resolve gives no relation; and the conformers check is held back, with unused-api and orphaned, when the index misses a non-test file of the language.'
  rationale: 'Closes gap G261115-g8qi8: inheritance clauses were resolved by name even with a complete index, so a conformer of one Store protocol was reported for another with the same name. Resolving through the index follows from ''never matches by name''; the rule now says so and names conformers among the held-back checks, since a conformer in a missed file can''t be resolved. Rejected: holding the check back whenever any clause entry is unresolved, because external supertypes (View, Codable) have no in-repo target and would hold it back in every repository; and falling back to name resolution for unresolved entries, which CORE-003 forbids. Index readers emit line-level references only, so the name in the clause picks which of the header''s references belongs to which entry; the declaration is still the one the index names.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-003
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261116-tnmgj
  timestamp: 2026-11-16T21:35:41Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-16T21:36:33Z
  summary: 'Change CORE-003: a language with no changed file in the review is not indexed. It ends syntax-only, like a language whose index could not be built, with an index-unavailable warning saying that none of its files changed. A language with one changed file anywhere in the repository is indexed as before.'
  rationale: 'Measured on 2026-11-17 (docs/rebuild-step5-speed.md, B5): a Rust-only change built the Swift index at both sides although no Swift file changed, 70 of 89 s from empty caches. A language with no changed file has no review items, so its structural checks have nothing to check, and with no references across languages in phase 1 its index cannot hide or reveal a caller of a changed item in another language; what is lost is call-level detail on edges among its unchanged components. TypeScript already skips its index this way, which CORE-003 did not list as a reason; this decision covers it. Rejected: keep building every language (costs about 70 s cold per review that leaves Swift untouched); skip per workspace or unit (would leave part of a changed language unindexed, so files would be missed and checks held back for no gain). The user chose this option in bean rm-gnyb.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-003
  retires: []
  terms: []
